\title{Exact Evaluation Oracles and Global Arithmetic Claims}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We analyze the mathematical power of an ideal exact evaluator that returns a requested value of a complex function in constant oracle time. Motivated by proposals that instantaneous evaluation of $L$-functions should settle zero conjectures, analytic-rank questions, functorial identities, distribution laws, and integer factorization, we separate pointwise evaluation from the information required for global conclusions. The central negative result is a finite-observation interpolation theorem: for any function $f:\mathbb C\to\mathbb C$, finite sample $S$, fresh point $z\notin S$, and prescribed value $T$, a polynomial perturbation agrees with $f$ on $S$ and takes value $T$ at $z$. Thus a finite exact transcript cannot exclude an unseen zero or determine unrestricted global behavior. We also prove positive transfer principles for one-query reductions, establish uniqueness and bounded discovery of the first nonzero jet coefficient, and identify the divisibility certificate that turns an oracle-derived decoder into a valid factor-search procedure. The framework replaces informal claims of omnipotence by precise hypotheses: global certificates, effective bounds, restricted-family rigidity, and certified arithmetic decoders.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

An $L$-function packages arithmetic information into a complex analytic object. Zeros of the Riemann zeta function are linked to the distribution of primes; the central behavior of an elliptic-curve $L$-function is conjecturally linked to the rank of the curve; Euler factors encode local arithmetic data; and identities of automorphic $L$-functions express instances of functorial transfer. This concentration of information invites an oracle thought experiment: suppose any value $L(s)$ could be returned exactly in $O(1)$ oracle time. What would follow?

Several distinct conclusions are often conflated. Point evaluation can decide whether a specified argument is a zero, whereas the Riemann Hypothesis classifies every nontrivial zero in an unbounded region. Exact Taylor coefficients identify the first nonzero coefficient after it is encountered, but an algorithm needs a stopping guarantee. Exact local factors can contribute to factoring only if a proved decoder converts them into a proper divisor. Equality at finitely many points can suggest equality of structured functions, but the inference requires a converse or rigidity theorem. An oracle may collapse classes relative to itself without proving an unconditional separation such as $P\ne NP$.

We develop a minimal framework for these distinctions. The negative side is information-theoretic: finite values of an unrestricted complex function leave arbitrary freedom at unsampled points. The positive side is reduction-theoretic and certificate-based: once a decision predicate, nonvanishing bound, or arithmetic decoder is supplied, evaluation composes with it transparently.

The conclusions are conditional and exact. Standard $L$-functions do not form an unrestricted family. Rather, the interpolation obstruction shows that any valid local-to-global inference must use the structure distinguishing them from arbitrary functions. It locates the substantive work in prospective oracle arguments.

\section{Evaluation oracles and decision problems}

Let $Q$ be a query space, $A$ an answer space, and $E:Q\to A$ an exact evaluator. An element of $Q$ may contain a description of a function and an evaluation point. Complexity claims depend on representation and cost model, but the extensional principles below do not.

\begin{definition}[One-query reducibility]
Let $X$ be an input space and $D\subseteq X$. The problem $D$ is \emph{one-query reducible} to $E$ if there are a query map $q:X\to Q$ and an acceptance predicate $R:A\to\{\mathrm{true},\mathrm{false}\}$ such that
\[
x\in D\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad R(E(q(x)))=\mathrm{true}
\]
for every $x\in X$.
\end{definition}

The definition separates encoding, evaluation, and interpretation. It assigns no hidden complexity bound to encoding or interpretation.

\begin{theorem}[Fiber reduction]
For every predicate $R$ on $A$, the output fiber
\[
D_R=\{q\in Q:R(E(q))=\mathrm{true}\}
\]
is one-query reducible to $E$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Use the identity as query map and $R$ as acceptance predicate. Membership in $D_R$ is the asserted equivalence by definition.
\end{proof}

Given $D\subseteq X$ and $T\subseteq Y$, a map $e:X\to Y$ is a many-one reduction when $x\in D$ if and only if $e(x)\in T$.

\begin{theorem}[Composition with many-one preprocessing]
Suppose $D\subseteq X$ many-one reduces to $T\subseteq Y$, and $T$ is one-query reducible to $E$. Then $D$ is one-query reducible to $E$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $e:X\to Y$ be the many-one encoding, and let $q:Y\to Q$ and $R$ witness the oracle reduction of $T$. The composite query is $q\circ e$, since
\[
x\in D\Longleftrightarrow e(x)\in T
\Longleftrightarrow R(E(q(e(x))))=\mathrm{true}.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Oracle-class collapse through a hard target]
Let $\mathcal C$ be a class of problems and let $T$ be a target to which every $D\in\mathcal C$ many-one reduces. If $T$ is one-query reducible to $E$, then every $D\in\mathcal C$ is one-query reducible to $E$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply the composition theorem to each $D\in\mathcal C$.
\end{proof}

This is the precise content of an oracle-relative collapse. It implies no unconditional equality or inequality of ordinary complexity classes. Ordinary polynomial time additionally requires polynomial-time query construction and interpretation, polynomial query length, and access to the oracle in the stated model. A strong oracle does not contradict a time-hierarchy theorem, because oracle access changes the model.

\section{The finite-observation interpolation obstruction}

For a finite set $S\subset\mathbb C$, define
\[
P_S(w)=\prod_{a\in S}(w-a).
\]
For $S=\varnothing$, this product is $1$.

\begin{lemma}[Vanishing on the sample]
If $w\in S$, then $P_S(w)=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The product contains the factor $w-w=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nonvanishing off the sample]
If $z\notin S$, then $P_S(z)\ne0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every factor $z-a$ is nonzero, and a finite product of nonzero complex numbers is nonzero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-observation interpolation obstruction]
Let $f:\mathbb C\to\mathbb C$, let $S\subset\mathbb C$ be finite, let $z\notin S$, and let $T\in\mathbb C$. There is a function $g:\mathbb C\to\mathbb C$ such that $g(w)=f(w)$ for every $w\in S$ and $g(z)=T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $P_S(z)\ne0$, put
\[
\lambda=\frac{T-f(z)}{P_S(z)},\qquad
 g(w)=f(w)+\lambda P_S(w).
\]
If $w\in S$, then $P_S(w)=0$, so $g(w)=f(w)$. At the fresh point,
\[
g(z)=f(z)+\frac{T-f(z)}{P_S(z)}P_S(z)=T.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[A finite transcript cannot exclude a fresh zero]
For every $z\notin S$, some $g$ agrees with $f$ on $S$ and satisfies $g(z)=0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Take $T=0$ in the preceding theorem.
\end{proof}

The perturbation is polynomial and remains entire when $f$ is entire, but need not preserve functional equations, Euler products, growth conditions, or membership in an $L$-function family. This is the point: successful finite-query theorems for restricted families must invoke restrictions that rule out such perturbations.

\subsection{Zeros, equality, and distributions}

A point evaluator decides $f(z)=0$ for a specified $z$ when equality is decidable in the answer representation. It cannot, from finitely many values alone, prove the absence of zeros at all unqueried points. The Riemann Hypothesis asserts
\[
\zeta(\rho)=0\ \text{and $\rho$ nontrivial}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \operatorname{Re}(\rho)=\frac12.
\]
A certified argument-principle calculation may count zeros in a bounded rectangle, but it needs contour control, approximation bounds, and a certificate that the contour is zero-free. Unbounded height requires further global control.

Likewise, finite agreement does not imply equality of two holomorphic functions; the identity theorem requires agreement on a set with an accumulation point. Finite local comparisons establish functorial identity only with a quantitative converse theorem for a restricted arithmetic family.

Distribution laws concern tails. For local quantities $x_p$, a statement such as
\[
\lim_{X\to\infty}
\frac{\#\{p\le X:x_p\in I\}}{\#\{p\le X\}}=\mu(I)
\]
cannot follow from a finite prefix alone. Effective discrepancy or tail bounds are the missing bridge.

\section{Orders of vanishing and bounded jets}

Let $c:\mathbb N\to\mathbb C$ be a Taylor or derivative jet. For an analytic $F$ near $s_0$, one may take $c_k=F^{(k)}(s_0)/k!$.

\begin{definition}[First nonzero jet index]
An index $k$ is first nonzero when $c_k\ne0$ and $c_j=0$ for every $j<k$. For a nonzero analytic function this is its finite order of vanishing at $s_0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness]
A jet has at most one first nonzero index.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $k<m$ were both first nonzero, the condition at $m$ would give $c_k=0$, contradicting $c_k\ne0$. The case $m<k$ is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-jet existence under bounded nonvanishing]
Let $B\in\mathbb N$. If $c_k\ne0$ for some $k\le B$, then there is a unique first nonzero index $k\le B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The finite set
\[
N_B=\{k\in\{0,1,\ldots,B\}:c_k\ne0\}
\]
is nonempty. Its least element is nonzero and every earlier coefficient vanishes by minimality. Uniqueness follows from the preceding theorem.
\end{proof}

Thus exact coefficient access and a valid bound $B$ yield a search using at most $B+1$ queries. The essential arithmetic input is the bound. For elliptic curves, relating the resulting analytic rank to algebraic rank requires the separate bridge asserted by the Birch--Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture.

\section{Certified factor extraction}

\begin{definition}[Factor-search specification]
A function $F:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ is a valid factor-search procedure if, for every composite $n\ge2$,
\[
F(n)\mid n,\qquad 1<F(n)<n.
\]
No condition is imposed on prime inputs, and complexity is separate from this extensional condition.
\end{definition}

Let $q:\mathbb N\to Q$ construct an oracle query and let $d:\mathbb N\times A\to\mathbb N$ decode an answer. Define $F(n)=d(n,E(q(n)))$.

\begin{theorem}[Certified oracle decoder]
Assume that for every composite $n\ge2$, the number $d(n,E(q(n)))$ divides $n$ and lies strictly between $1$ and $n$. Then $F$ is a valid factor-search procedure.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
After substituting the definition of $F$, the hypotheses are exactly the three requirements in the factor-search specification.
\end{proof}

For polynomial-time factoring, one must also prove that $q(n)$ has polynomial length in $\log n$, query construction and decoding are polynomial-time, and the answer has a usable representation. A candidate divisor is cheaply certified by checking $n\bmod d=0$ and $1<d<n$; the hard part is proving that the proposed oracle pipeline always produces one on composite inputs.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Finite-transcript perturbation.}
Given samples $S$, a fresh point $z$, and target $T$, compute $P_S(z)$ and $\lambda=(T-f(z))/P_S(z)$. Return the evaluator $g(w)=f(w)+\lambda P_S(w)$. Naive evaluation takes $O(|S|)$ complex multiplications. This demonstrates non-identifiability; it is not asserted to preserve an arithmetic family.

\paragraph{Bounded first-nonzero search.}
Given exact access to $c_k$ and a valid $B$, query $c_0,c_1,\ldots,c_B$ in order and return the first nonzero index. The method uses at most $B+1$ queries and $O(B)$ equality tests. Correctness follows from bounded nonvanishing and well-ordering.

\paragraph{Certified factor decoding.}
Construct $q(n)$, obtain $a=E(q(n))$, decode $d=d(n,a)$, and verify $n\bmod d=0$ and $1<d<n$. Failure of verification means the pipeline has not met its specification. A completeness theorem guaranteeing success on every composite input turns the pipeline into a factor-search method.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\subsection{The Riemann Hypothesis}
Exact evaluation decides whether a specified $s$ is a zero. It does not establish a universal classification. An oracle-assisted proof needs certified zero counts on regions and a method covering all heights.

\subsection{Birch--Swinnerton-Dyer}
Central jet access locates the first nonzero coefficient once a finite search bound is known. A single value distinguishes only order zero from positive order. Identifying analytic and algebraic rank remains a separate mathematical assertion.

\subsection{Sato--Tate}
Exact local coefficients give exact finite histograms, not convergence to the Sato--Tate measure. Effective discrepancy estimates are required to control the unseen tail.

\subsection{Langlands functoriality}
Finite local agreement certifies a lift only when a converse rigidity theorem supplies a sufficient threshold within the relevant bounded family.

\subsection{Complexity classes}
A class whose members reduce to an oracle-decidable hard target collapses to one evaluator query relative to that evaluator. This is compatible with either $P=NP$ or $P\ne NP$ in the ordinary model.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The framework organizes local-to-global bridges into six types:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item zero statements require certified region counts or exclusion bounds;
\item vanishing orders require a finite nonvanishing bound;
\item distribution laws require tail or discrepancy estimates;
\item global identities require rigidity in a restricted family;
\item factor search requires a correct decoder and divisibility certificate;
\item complexity collapse requires explicit reductions and an honest cost model.
\end{enumerate}

The interpolation theorem shows that these are substantive rather than cosmetic. Without restrictions, finite observations determine no fresh value. Conversely, once the bridge is supplied, evaluation plays a clean compositional role.

Future research should seek polynomial-size zero-count certificates for completed $L$-functions; conductor-dependent bounds for central Taylor jets of elliptic curves; auxiliary arithmetic objects with complete, certified factor decoders; quantitative converse-rigidity thresholds for bounded automorphic families; effective discrepancy estimates for coefficient distributions; and oracle-relative hierarchy theorems recording query budgets, preprocessing costs, answer sizes, and adaptivity.

\section{Conclusion}

Instant exact evaluation is powerful but local. It does not alone classify all zeros, determine an unbounded vanishing order, prove a limiting distribution, identify global representations from finite samples, or extract factors. A finite transcript is compatible with arbitrary behavior at a fresh point. Positive consequences arise after adding explicit structure: one-query reductions compose with many-one encodings, bounded nonvanishing makes jet search finite, and a certified decoder turns oracle output into factor search. The decisive research problem is the construction of certificates, bounds, reductions, and rigidity principles that transform local values into global arithmetic knowledge.

\end{document}

